Chapter 8
I begin my next day with cleaning up my dishevelled apartment while listening to the news on my phone.
(t): I wanted to watch the news on TV, but it is beyond saving. Looks like I need to splurge on a new one soon.
The news reveals that the country will have to pay a large sum to cover the costs of reparations and rebuilding. It's a miracle that there weren't any casualties.
Time flies by, and when I'm finished with restoring my living space to the best of my abilities, I realise that the sun has already set.
(t): Whew, I'm spent now. Tomorrow I will just go shopping and relax, then everything will be as good as before.
I take out the trash with all the broken things that can't be used anymore and make myself ready for bed.
Oh right, I should eat dinner.
I pop a frozen pizza in the oven for a quick meal and go on my phone while it's baking.
I notice that I have a new email in my inbox and open it.
(t): It's from the company!
Wait, don't tell me…
I read the email and am overcome with disappointment by the end of it.
It says that work will continue tomorrow because the building and everything inside suffered no damage aside from a few computers that have already been replaced.
(t): Ugh, I thought I could take it easy for a little while, but nooo.
I eat my pizza and sullenly go to sleep early so I can wake up on time tomorrow for work.
(t): It really feels like the earthquake didn't happen at all. At least while I'm here at work.
I'm impressed that Miss Moon managed to get everything back on its track so quickly, it shows how dedicated she is to her company.
(t): That reminds me, I haven't seen her at all today. She must be cooped up in her office right now, working as always.
CHOICE OPTION 1: Alright! Let's work hard too!
Influenced by my admiration of Miss Moon's work ethic I decide to throw myself into work as well and finish every task as efficiently as possible.
I even go as far as helping some of my coworkers.
(t): Wow, I don't know where this sudden energy boost came from, but it feels kind of rewarding seeing the thankful smiles of my coworkers.
I remember how they were on my first day here. I'm sure I was able to get on their good sides today.
(t): I hope we'll get along even better from now on!
But my hopes are in vain.
I manage to get everything done in time. I even have 30 minutes left.
(t): Shall I leave a little early?
Just then a coworker approaches me.
Shy coworker: Hi. I'm sorry to ask this of you, but could you finish this task for me? It's just sorting these files of vendors and clients.
She uncomfortably furrows her brows.
Shy coworker: I hate to do this, as I'm sure you have enough on your plate as is, but I just got a call from my son's school. They say it's an emergency.
I look at the stack of paper in her hand.
(t): It's not too much. I think I can manage that in more or less ten minutes. I just have to make sure that the right client file is attached to the right vendor list.
Me: It's really no problem. It's not that big of a task, and you should be by your son's side. Don't worry, I got you.
(t): Even if I do this, I will still be early. I won't be able to go shopping for a new TV, but at least I'm doing a good deed.
The coworker thanks me and hurries off afterwards.
I'm feeling good about myself and begin working on sorting the papers.
Two minutes later I notice that the stack has gotten bigger.
(t): Huh? But how can that be?
I look closer and see that there has been another stack added on top of the one I was working on.
Shameless coworker: Sorry I added that one. I hope you understand. I have an emergency too, and I noticed that you so readily helped out someone else just now.
He already packed everything, ready to leave.
(t): Ugh, I mean, he wasn't very polite, but what's one more task?…
Me: It's fine … I guess.
Shameless coworker: Thanks! I owe you one.
As time goes by I find myself accepting more and more little tasks from other people, and it's starting to pile up.
(t): Damn, why can't I just say no?
(t): But I don't want to make them dislike me.
That's how I end up staying late in the office.
(t): I need to finish this all before I go home, but after today I swear I will never do any favours for anyone ever again!
I look around and notice that I'm the last person left.
With a heavy heart I continue working.
It's already close to midnight.
(t): I should just focus and pull through with this.
I come across a vendor research document that needs Miss Moon's approval, but the person who left it with me didn't get it.
(t): I'm not sure if she's even still here.
But I still go to Miss Moon's office and knock on her door.
(t): Who knows, maybe she's such a workaholic that she decides to work overtime voluntarily.
A voice from inside answers.
Miss Moon: Come in.
I enter her office, surprised that she really is still here.
Miss Moon: {playerName}? I'm surprised you're staying late. What brings you here?
Me: Um, to be honest, I finished my work a long time ago, but I ended up helping a few people out, so it turned out like this.
She raises an eyebrow.
Miss Moon: Just so you know, being a pushover won't help you in life.
(t): Yeah, I know. I experienced it today.
I just smile politely and nod.
Me: This document still needs your approval, ma'am.
CHOICE OPTION 2: Couldn't be me. I need a break already.
I manage to pull through the majority of the day, but sometime in the afternoon I decide to take a break and get a coffee.
(t): I just can't believe that Miss Moon would make us work a day after the earthquake. I'm still shaken up by the event.
I decide to spend the next 30 minutes in the break room and sip my coffee with enjoyment.
Then the door suddenly opens, and the person I didn't want to show up the most enters the room.
Miss Moon: There you are.
I sit up straight immediately.
Me: Yes, I was just taking a quick break.
She looks at me doubtfully.
Miss Moon: For half an hour? The others confirmed that you've been gone for quite some time now.
(t): Tsk. Those traitors.
Me: I apologise, Miss Moon. I just have a lot on my mind right now. With the earthquake that happened and all…
Miss Moon: I don't understand why you're so unmotivated right now. Did someone close to you get hurt by the earthquake, or did something of the sort happen?
Me: No, everything is okay, just some furniture that needs to be replaced.
Miss Moon: Then I really don't understand. The earthquake was yesterday. It didn't have any significant effects for us. So why?
Me: I … I just … I don't know. I'm sorry. I will get my act together.
(t): Am I the only one that finds this a bit too hectic right now? It's true. Everyone else doesn't seem to mind work right now.
Miss Moon: You're still relatively new here, so I expect you to show me that I didn't make a mistake in hiring you.
(t): I guess she has a point.
Ashamed I look down on the floor.
Me: Yes ma'am, I'll do my best.
Miss Moon: Then show it. Finish the extra work I left on your desk by the end of today. And understand that I do this because I care about the company and its staff.
(t): So she's saying this is my punishment for slacking off for half an hour and that I'll lose my job if I can't prove that I'm hard-working.
I already dread the sight of my desk and just nod.
Miss Moon finally leaves the room, and I breathe a sigh of relief as all the tension leaves my body.
(t): I don't know what's wrong with me. Why can't I get it together? Now I got scolded by the office fury.
I make my way back to the office, and my knees weaken even further at the sight of the amount of paper towering on my desk.
(t): If I'm to finish all this by the end of today, I'll have to work until late in the evening.
I see some of my coworkers giving me pitying looks or slightly shaking their heads with disbelief at the amount of paper stacks on my desk.
(t): I bet they're glad that they're not in my shoes right now.
Dejected, I start to work myself through all the different tasks.
After a time that feels like a decade of sorting through clients' contracts and vendor lists as well as doing research on different types of venues and writing invitation templates, I finally see the mountain of papers diminish.
I look at the clock and see that it's almost midnight and everyone has left a long time ago.
(t): Phew. All that's left now is to get Miss Moon's approval on this research document and then the end-of-day report.
I feel my body screaming at me because I've been sitting like a shrimp while working.
I stretch my back before gathering the needed papers and heading to Miss Moon's office.
(t): I wonder if she's still here. I'm kind of scared. What if she gives me even more work?
I knock and open the door after waiting a few seconds.
Of course she's still here. She's the definition of a workaholic.
Miss Moon speaks without looking up from her work.
Miss Moon: Did you manage to finish everything? If so, that's very impressive. I'll give you that.
I walk over and present her with the document.
I am tired but also kind of proud because she just complimented me.
Me: Yes ma'am, everything is done. I only need your approval for this document, and I'll finish up for today.
Miss Moon: Right, let's see.
CONTINUE HERE AFTER CHOICE OPTIONS
Miss Moon takes the document, reads over it quickly and shakes her head.
Miss Moon: This will not get my approval. It needs to be revised. I wish people wouldn't bring me this kind of sloppy work.
Something snaps inside of me, probably because I've worked too much today.
(t): Why does she have to say it with that attitude? It's not my fault!
Me: Well, then I can't finish this task. I shall leave that to you.
(t): Do it yourself then if you're so much better!
Miss Moon: I appreciate you working this late, but I ask you to remain professional while in the office.
(t): You're the one with an attitude!
Me: Pardon me? I believe I've done nothing wrong this evening.
Miss Moon: I can read your expressions like a book. You can't hide your disdain for me. And also, anyone with a working pair of eyes can see that this florist here is a mismatch because the bride requested Juliet Roses specifically.
(t): So what? I couldn't be bothered to double-check because I had my hands full with other documents.
Me: What seems to be the problem?
I press my lips into a forced smile and try my best to come across as polite as possible. Internally I'm fuming, though.
Miss Moon sighs with disappointment.
Miss Moon: Juliet roses are very rarely sold because they require meticulous treatment and thus are very pricey. That's why only the most high-end florists sell them.
Me: I didn't—
Miss Moon stands up and positions herself directly in front of me just to push the document back into my hands.
Miss Moon: I should have known better than to expect this much of you. Redo this first thing tomorrow morning.
(t): This is so unfair! And she's just so ruthless. It really hurts.
I feel my chest aching.
Miss Moon: You may leave now.
I see my vision getting blurry.
(t): Her words do hurt me, but this feels a bit excessive. Something's not right.
Miss Moon: {playerName}? What's the matter?
I slowly start losing my balance and feel myself falling backwards.
(t): Oh, I think I've reached my limits.
I close my eyes and hear Miss Moon yelping. I brace myself mentally for the impact on the back of my head, but instead I feel something grabbing hold of my arm, and then I hear a faint thud.
ILLUSTRATION
Miss Moon: {playerName}, are you okay?
I open my eyes slowly and see my boss' face right in front of mine.
My head doesn't hurt. She must have cushioned my fall with her arm.
(t): Her face. It's so captivating. And close.
I notice how worried she looks.
Miss Moon: Can you hear me?
I don't have the energy to answer her.
My eyes fixate on the big scar on her face.
(t): I never noticed it was this big. I want to know what happened to her.
I unconsciously reach for the scar with my fingertips.
I remember her flinching slightly before everything turns black.
MINIGAME
